1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,679
Hi everyone! Welcome to Passport English, making English easy and fun. I'm J.T.

2
00:00:05,679 --> 00:00:10,400
Yay! I'm Maddie, and I'm so excited for today.

3
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:15,679
Me too. Today we're talking about small daily routines that help you speak English confidently.

4
00:00:15,679 --> 00:00:22,559
Oh yes. I know so many learners feel nervous to speak, but little steps every day can really help.

5
00:00:22,559 --> 00:00:28,400
Exactly. And I think everyone thinks they need grammar books, but that's not true.

6
00:00:28,399 --> 00:00:33,759
Totally. Speaking is about practice, not rules. Even talking to yourself counts.

7
00:00:35,359 --> 00:00:39,759
I do that all the time. Sometimes I practice dialogues in my car.

8
00:00:39,759 --> 00:00:45,840
Same. Or when I'm cooking, I repeat phrases or sentences out loud. It feels silly, but it works.

9
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:52,239
That's right. And small routines don't have to take long. Even five minutes a day can make a

10
00:00:52,239 --> 00:00:58,159
difference. Oh yes. Consistency is key. I tell my learners, pick a short routine and do it

11
00:00:58,159 --> 00:01:05,200
every day. Exactly. For example, you could describe your day each morning. Like, I drink coffee,

12
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:12,799
I go to work, and so on. Yes. And don't worry about mistakes. Just speak. Your brain will get

13
00:01:12,799 --> 00:01:18,319
used to it. And the more you speak, the more confident you feel. It's like building a muscle.

14
00:01:18,319 --> 00:01:24,799
Totally agree. Small steps, everyday practice, and fun exercises make learning enjoyable.

15
00:01:24,799 --> 00:01:31,039
Absolutely. And today we'll share three simple routines that you can start right now.

16
00:01:31,039 --> 00:01:36,319
Yay! Let's do it. Ready, everyone? Grab a notebook or just listen and repeat with us.

17
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:42,719
Routine one. Morning self-talk. When you wake up, try speaking out loud about your plans.

18
00:01:42,719 --> 00:01:49,200
Yes. Say things like, I will have breakfast. I will go to work. I will practice English.

19
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:54,400
And don't just think it. Speak it. Even one or two sentences are enough.

20
00:01:55,040 --> 00:01:59,920
Oh yes. I do that while brushing my teeth or making coffee. It only takes a few minutes.

21
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:07,120
Sometimes I even speak to my reflection in the mirror. Feel silly at first, but it works.

22
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:11,360
Totally. Your brain starts to connect words with speaking habits.

23
00:02:12,240 --> 00:02:20,880
Routine two. Describe your surroundings. Anywhere you are at home, at work, outside, just narrate what you see.

24
00:02:20,880 --> 00:02:27,840
For example, I see a cup of coffee. The sun is shining. My cat is sleeping. Simple, but effective.

25
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:33,200
And try to use full sentences instead of single words. That trains your speaking flow.

26
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:39,440
Oh yes. When I do this on the bus, people think I'm crazy. But it really helps me practice.

27
00:02:39,840 --> 00:02:46,079
Haha. Me too. People staring don't matter. Your confidence grows faster than embarrassment.

28
00:02:46,079 --> 00:02:52,639
Absolutely. And you can add feelings too. I feel happy. I feel relaxed. It makes it more natural.

29
00:02:52,639 --> 00:02:56,159
Great tip. It's about expressing yourself, not being perfect.

30
00:02:56,800 --> 00:03:00,319
Routine three. Many dialogues with yourself or a friend.

31
00:03:01,199 --> 00:03:06,159
Yes. Pretend someone asks you questions and you answer. Like a mini interview.

32
00:03:06,159 --> 00:03:13,120
Example. What did you do yesterday? I watched a movie and practiced English. What movie? A comedy movie.

33
00:03:13,120 --> 00:03:18,719
It was funny. Perfect. Even three to five questions a day build speaking stamina.

34
00:03:18,719 --> 00:03:24,800
And if you have a friend or language partner, even better. You get real-time feedback and interaction.

35
00:03:25,519 --> 00:03:32,560
But even alone, you can practice tone, intonation and rhythm. TTS will do the rest if you listen back.

36
00:03:32,879 --> 00:03:41,120
Yes. Listening and repeating is key. Now, let's talk about common challenges learners face.

37
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:48,800
Oh yes. First challenge. Fear of making mistakes. Exactly. Many learners stay quiet because they are

38
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:55,759
afraid of being wrong. But mistakes are part of learning. In fact, they are your fastest way to improve.

39
00:03:56,159 --> 00:04:01,599
Totally agree. I always tell learners. Speak first, correct later.

40
00:04:01,599 --> 00:04:07,199
And notice this. Your brain will adapt over time. We'll speak faster and more naturally.

41
00:04:07,759 --> 00:04:14,000
Second challenge. Not enough speaking opportunities. Yes. If you only read and listen,

42
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:21,120
your speaking stays weak. You need practice every day. Even five to ten minutes daily can make a huge

43
00:04:21,120 --> 00:04:27,600
difference. It's better than one long practice per week. Oh yes. And don't forget online communities.

44
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:33,360
Language exchange apps or talking to yourself. Third challenge. Getting stuck on vocabulary.

45
00:04:34,240 --> 00:04:40,560
Don't worry. Use simple words first. Don't try to sound fancy. If you don't know a word,

46
00:04:40,560 --> 00:04:44,800
describe it. Example. I'm holding a round theme to drink coffee.

47
00:04:45,920 --> 00:04:49,920
Yes. Your brain will remember it faster and you still keep talking.

48
00:04:50,720 --> 00:04:54,640
Let's move on to tips for more fun and engagement.

49
00:04:54,640 --> 00:05:02,400
Tip one. Use your favorite topics. Speak about movies, food, hobbies, anything you love.

50
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:05,840
Yes. Passion makes your speech flow naturally.

51
00:05:06,720 --> 00:05:14,240
Tip two. Add emotions and expressions. Laugh, react, or exclaim. It makes your speech lively.

52
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:21,120
Tip three. Record yourself. Listen and repeat. You'll notice improvement every day.

53
00:05:21,120 --> 00:05:24,879
And it's motivating. You hear your progress.

54
00:05:25,519 --> 00:05:34,240
Another fun tip. Shadowing TTS or podcasts. Yes. Listen to a short sentence. Pause and repeat

55
00:05:34,240 --> 00:05:41,360
exactly like the speaker. Match tone and rhythm. This trains pronunciation, intonation, and natural flow.

56
00:05:41,360 --> 00:05:47,280
It's super effective. And you can do it anywhere. On the bus, at home, or during a walk.

57
00:05:48,480 --> 00:05:55,840
Okay. Let's talk about weekly progress routines. Yes. Pick three to five small daily exercises

58
00:05:55,840 --> 00:06:02,720
and stick to them for a week. Example. Morning self-talk. Describing surroundings. Many dialogues.

59
00:06:02,720 --> 00:06:09,920
Record or repeat. At the end of the week, reflect on what improved. And make it fun. Don't stress

60
00:06:09,920 --> 00:06:17,680
about perfection. Lastly, let's discuss motivation tips. Oh yes. Set small goals every day.

61
00:06:17,680 --> 00:06:24,879
Speak for five minutes. Learn three new expressions. Celebrate small wins. Every step counts.

62
00:06:24,879 --> 00:06:30,960
And remember, consistency beats intensity. Five minutes every day, one hour, once a week.

63
00:06:31,759 --> 00:06:38,480
Exactly. That's how real progress happens. All right. It's time for our word tour.

64
00:06:38,480 --> 00:06:43,680
Today, we'll look at five useful words. We'll spell them, explain them, and use them.

65
00:06:43,680 --> 00:06:47,360
Let's start with the first one. The word is confident.

66
00:06:47,360 --> 00:06:51,120
C-O-N-F-I-D-E-N-T.

67
00:06:51,120 --> 00:06:57,439
I love this word. Confident means you feel sure about yourself. You believe you can do something well.

68
00:06:58,080 --> 00:07:04,080
Not perfect. Just sure. For example, I feel confident when I practice English every day.

69
00:07:04,079 --> 00:07:07,599
Yes. Exactly. Confidence grows when you practice.

70
00:07:08,240 --> 00:07:13,919
All right. Word number two. Routine. R-O-U-T-I-N-E

71
00:07:13,919 --> 00:07:20,079
A routine is something you do regularly, usually every day or every week, like drinking coffee in the

72
00:07:20,079 --> 00:07:27,120
morning or brushing your teeth. Example, my morning routine includes practicing English for five minutes.

73
00:07:27,199 --> 00:07:34,959
Nice. Word number three is expression. E-X-P-R-E-S-S-I-O-N

74
00:07:35,280 --> 00:07:41,920
An expression is a way to show your thoughts or feelings. You can use words, your voice, or even your

75
00:07:41,920 --> 00:07:46,959
face. For example, adding facial expressions makes your English more natural.

76
00:07:46,959 --> 00:07:55,680
Very important for speaking. Okay. Word number four. Consistent. C-O-N-S-I-S-T-E-N-T

77
00:07:55,680 --> 00:08:02,240
Consistent means doing something again and again without stopping. Not one day, but many days.

78
00:08:02,879 --> 00:08:07,280
Example, being consistent with your practice helps you improve quickly.

79
00:08:07,280 --> 00:08:14,319
And our final word today is engaging. E-N-G-A-G-I-N-G

80
00:08:14,319 --> 00:08:19,519
Engaging means interesting and enjoyable. It makes people want to pay attention.

81
00:08:20,079 --> 00:08:23,680
For example, an engaging conversation is easier to follow.

82
00:08:24,479 --> 00:08:31,599
Great job. Now try using these five words today. Even one sentence per word is enough.

